    "debrief":  {
                    "realTeam":  "On September 10, 2017, the Sun released a huge flare. On the surface of Mars, the rover Curiosity measured radiation about double the normal level for about two days. The MAVEN spacecraft saw an aurora around the whole planet, more than 25 times brighter than usual. Scientists use storms like this to plan how future astronauts will stay safe.",
                    "tryNext":  "Learn the way to the shelter on a quiet sol, then go as soon as the storm alert comes. Every sol you spend sheltered keeps your dose low.",
                    "realScience":  {
                                        "title":  "How to hide from radiation",
                                        "text":  "Thick walls, soil and water all block radiation. Future Mars bases might pile Martian soil (regolith) on top of shelters, or put water tanks around the bedrooms, to keep astronauts safe during solar storms.",
                                        "glossary":  "shielding"
                                    },
